            <ArticleTitle>Pro-MMP-9/TIMP-1 ratio correlates poorly with a direct assessment of MMP-9 activity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1480-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinbiochem.2011.09.019</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine if the pro-MMP-9/TIMP-1 ratio is an accurate surrogate for endogenously active MMP-9 levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Plasma active MMP-9, pro-MMP-9 and TIMP-1 were measured in 295 patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was a weak negative correlation between the pro-MMP-9/TIMP-1 ratio and active MMP-9. TIMP-1 was more closely correlated with active MMP-9 than pro-MMP-9.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pro-MMP-9/TIMP-1 ratio measured with ELISA is not a good surrogate measure for active MMP-9, and direct measurements of active MMP-9 are therefore recommended.</AbstractText>
